  "body": "Found while working #982 (branch `issue/982-microlift-acceptance`; decision record `docs/decisions/982-microlift-acceptance.md`).\n\n**What happens.** When a developed neck band of `rotary_adaptive`'s bars is HSM-planned (kept in cut by microlift returns), two readings on the suite's 0.1 mm dexel stand past the laws the whole r7 bar is pinned to:\n\n1. **Bore exit load.** The r6.2 bar's levels 5.7 and 5.2 both read **20.3 % D** (55.5 deg) at one move, the first turn out of the entry's bore (developed (21.7, 7.7) -> (20.8, 6.8) on 5.7). The law for the r7 bar is optimal + 35 % = 19.8 %. The planner's own 0.05 mm stock reads 47.5 deg there and names no overload; the r7 bar's lifted level 5.5 reads 53.0 deg (18.4 %) at the same place.\n2. **In-cut breaks at the band's west end.** Level 5.2 of the r6.2 bar posts 3 breaks of 5.1, 5.5 and 5.5 deg near developed (-15.9, 4.7); level 5.0 of the r7 bar, when it is planned (see the budget knife edge, #1051), posts 5 breaks of 5.0-6.2 deg near (-18.3, 5.5). They are on the spiral's own chords (a piece cut on longer chords hides them some of the time).\n\n**Why it was not seen.** Until #982 these bands were refused for travel on most runs (the microlift acceptance flipped on a sliver), so the plans were rarely posted and never pinned. #982's PR pins the r6.2 levels at what they read (`R62_LOAD_FACTOR` 1.40, `R62_BREAKS` 3 in `crates/motion/tests/rotary_adaptive.rs`) and names this issue.\n\n**What they replace.** Offset rings on the same bands: 100 % D and 15-21 breaks of 45-135 deg. So this is owed polish, not a regression.\n\nDone means: the r6.2 neck levels hold the whole bar's laws (dexel load <= optimal + 35 %, no in-cut break past 5 deg), `R62_LOAD_FACTOR` and `R62_BREAKS` are gone from the pin, and the r7 bar's level 5.0 holds them when it is planned.\n",
